  <div class="attributed-text-segment-list__container relative mt-1 mb-1.5 babybear:mt-0 babybear:mb-0.5">
    <p class="attributed-text-segment-list__content text-color-text !text-sm whitespace-pre-wrap break-words
        " dir="ltr" data-test-id="main-feed-activity-card__commentary">They had values on the wall, on the screensavers, in the onboarding packs, in the annual report.

What they didn't have was leaders who could tell you — in their own words, without looking at a slide — what those values actually meant for how they ran their teams.

Culture doesn't live in documents. It lives in daily decisions, in the conversations leaders choose to have... and avoid.

We worked with their leadership community to make the values real. Not as a communications exercise - as a leadership one.

The question we kept coming back to was: what would have to change about how you lead, for these values to actually be true here?

That's an uncomfortable question, but it's also the only one worth asking.

